# Go comments

Forestrie is a **highly distributed system**. The "why" of local code often
depends on adjacent apps or repositories. Comments must make that context
reachable without duplicating platform docs.

## Principles

1. **Signpost, don't silo.** At package and function level, state how the local
   code fits the wider picture: assumptions about other components, contracts
   honoured, and where to read more.
2. **Brief with a pointer.** One or two sentences plus a reference is enough
   in most cases. Only the most delicate interactions warrant longer prose.
3. **Explain purpose, not syntax.** Comments describe the **problem** or
   **role** of the following code. Restating what the code literally does is
   pointless.
4. **Non-obvious details only at line level.** Imperative logic a typical
   engineer would read straight through needs no comment. Line comments are for
   surprising mechanics, platform limits, ordering constraints, or wire-format
   quirks.
5. **Full coverage of exported surfaces.** Every **exported** function,
   method, type, constant, and variable must have a godoc comment describing its
   role. Document parameters and return semantics when not obvious from names
   and types.

## Package comments

Use `doc.go` (or the first file in the package) for a **package comment** that
states the service role, primary upstream/downstream dependencies, and links to
platform docs when the package sits on a cross-repo boundary (e.g. ranger queue
consumer ↔ canopy forestrie-ingress).

## Cross-repo and cross-service signposts

Prefer stable **GitHub blob links** with heading anchors:

```go
// ResolveLogSigningKey fetches the target log's active signer from Univocity
// public-root (GET /api/logs/{logId}/public-root). Grant verification model:
// https://github.com/forestrie/devdocs/blob/main/arc/arc-0019-grant-verification-model.md
```

| Target | Link form |
|--------|-----------|
| Same repo doc | `https://github.com/forestrie/arbor/blob/main/docs/…` |
| Platform ADR/ARC | `https://github.com/forestrie/devdocs/blob/main/arc/…` or `adr/…` |
| Sibling service | `https://github.com/forestrie/canopy/blob/main/packages/…` |
| On-chain behaviour | `https://github.com/forestrie/univocity/blob/main/docs/arc/…` |
| Ops runbooks | `https://github.com/forestrie/devdocs/blob/main/ops/…` |

Name the **remote component**, the **contract** (HTTP route, queue message,
COSE field, env var), and what this code **assumes** vs **guarantees**.

## What to write at each level

| Level | Comment answers |
|-------|-----------------|
| **Package** | Role in the distributed flow; primary dependencies and emitted side effects. |
| **Type** | Domain object or boundary; invariants callers must respect. |
| **Function / method** | Operation in the flow; preconditions, side effects, error semantics. |
| **Block** | Why this branch exists when the function comment is not enough. |
| **Line** | Non-obvious mechanics only. |

Good — wire-format constraint:

```go
// PullRequest is sent to POST /queue/pull on forestrie-ingress SequencingQueue.
// Fields are CBOR-tagged for SCRAPI worker compatibility.
type PullRequest struct {
```

Bad — restates syntax:

```go
// increment i and append to slice
for i := range items {
```

## Config and options structs

Document **config fields** on the **innermost function or constructor that
reads each field** — not at every callsite. Each documented field: meaning,
valid values or source, behaviour when missing or invalid.

## Godoc conventions

- Comments on exported symbols must start with the **symbol name**.
- Use complete sentences; wrap prose naturally (~79 chars is fine).
- Link to docs with bare URLs in godoc (markdown links are not rendered in
  `go doc`).
- Do not copy ARC/ADR prose into source — link and state the local obligation.

## When editing

- Behaviour crossing a repo or service boundary → add or update signpost
  comments in the same change.
- If an exported symbol lacks a comment, add one before merging.
